WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Here it is running.

2
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:08.760
The customer types what's going on, in her own words: married in the spring, a baby, renting, and hoping to buy.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.040
Baz doesn't reach for a product.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.780
It asks one question.

5
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.100
And it writes down what it learns, against a catalogue of facts, with where each one came from.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:25.200
A couple of answers later it has the shape of the thing: savings, what they can put away each month, when they'd like to buy.

7
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.260
It offers to keep that as a plan.

8
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.460
Nothing is kept until she taps.

9
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.060
That's the rule all the way through.

10
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:36.780
The model proposes, the customer commits, and the system checks before anything is written.

11
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.540
Now the products, and only now.

12
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.840
These are what a first home with a new baby usually involves, each with a reason tied to something she said.

13
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:50.080
She picks three, and three applications start at once, sharing what's already known.

14
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.640
Ask about rates and you get the published table.

15
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.200
Indicative, not advice.

16
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.040
She banks here already, so she signs in.

17
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.240
On this prototype that's an email code, not the bank's real identity check.

18
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.380
What the bank already holds arrives: name, address, PPS number, employer, salary.

19
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.680
Then Baz shows her what that saved.

20
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:14.240
Sixteen things she never had to type, and which application each one went to.

21
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.560
This is the part I'd want a bank to notice.

22
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.780
The personal loan uses the bank's own published form.

23
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.820
Baz has read the definition, four hundred and eighteen controls, and filled in what it knows.

24
00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:30.920
Now it asks for the rest in the form's own words: its declarations, its payslip, its photo ID.

25
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.960
The bank's machinery, driven by a conversation instead of a wizard.

26
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.300
One conversation, three applications, and here's where each one stands, straight from the database.

27
00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:47.540
Underneath, the application packet is built exactly as the bank's website would send it, every field with where its value came from.

28
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.240
Captured, never sent.

29
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.160
There's no code path in this prototype that sends it.

30
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.660
And the other side of the glass.

31
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.080
Every case, with every fact and where it came from.

32
00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.840
What each conversation is for.

33
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:02.680
The guardrails, which run in front of the model, not inside it.

34
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.080
The persona, which is style only.

35
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.940
And the numbers, from the events it wrote as it went.
